For Fedora, these are the main commands you'll use:

Update Fedora

sudo dnf upgrade --refresh

Install software

sudo dnf install package-name

Example:

sudo dnf install vlc

Remove software

sudo dnf remove package-name

Search for software

dnf search keyword

Example:

dnf search firefox

See installed packages

dnf list installed

Check Fedora version

cat /etc/fedora-release

or:

hostnamectl

System information

inxi -F

(Install it first if needed:)

sudo dnf install inxi

Disk information

lsblk

Check disk space

df -h

Check memory

free -h

Check running processes

top

or better:

htop

Install htop:

sudo dnf install htop

Restart / shut down

Restart:

systemctl reboot

Shutdown:

systemctl poweroff

Enable a service

sudo systemctl enable --now service-name

Example (SSH server):

sudo dnf install openssh-server
sudo systemctl enable --now sshd

Check hardware

lspci

USB devices:

lsusb

Network information

ip a

Wi-Fi status:

nmcli device

Fedora uses:

dnf = package manager (like Ubuntu's apt)

systemctl = services

journalctl = logs


Coming from Ubuntu, the biggest change is:

Ubuntu:

sudo apt update
sudo apt upgrade

Fedora:

sudo dnf upgrade --refresh

The rest of the Linux commands (ls, cd, cp, mv, grep, ssh, etc.) are mostly the same.